  collection-title: "IEEE International Conference on Intelligent Computer Communication and Processing (ICCP)"
  abstract: >-
    When integrating spatial data with river networks, does modelling the physical
    flow topology actually improve predictive power over simple proximity? Graph-
    based learning models are often evaluated without a connectivity-matched
    proximity baseline, so apparent improvements may reflect a larger neighbourhood
    rather than the underlying network structure. We introduce a matched-baseline
    evaluation protocol at national scale on two river networks. Germany (66,261
    reaches) pairs a 14.6 M-observation Sentinel-2 archive with a public but sparse
    in-situ chemistry record. Romania (18,692 reaches) has satellite coverage but no
    Sentinel-era in-situ chemistry. To our knowledge, this is the first controlled
    national-scale test of flow topology against connectivity-matched proximity in
    satellite water-quality monitoring. The satellite optical features provide a
    weak but usable screening proxy for pollution (ROC-AUC 0.60-0.62 for monthly
    threshold exceedances). Comparing the flow graph to a matched proximity baseline
    reveals a clear split in predictive utility. For static average pollution
    levels, explicit flow topology offers no measurable advantage (two Romanian
    exceptions aside), and a graph attention network does not overturn this null.
    For temporal dynamics in the optical signal, topology does add value. The
    clearest observational signal is temporal co-fluctuation: flow-connected reaches
    rise and fall together significantly more than equally close but flow-
    disconnected neighbours, a result that reproduces in both countries. Advantages
    in anomaly detection on partly circular synthetic injections are smaller, and
    the flow graph does not consistently improve source attribution, since pollution
    near a facility is mostly inherited from upstream.
